Glenn Gore: I'm going to just talk through auth, and I'll jump back and forth and kind of give some demos as we go through this.

99
00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:39.910
Glenn Gore: What you're going to see is the verifiable trust agent actually having some utility now. So, it started off as a key management service of just managing all your crypto keys.

100
00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:50.669
Glenn Gore: then it added DID management capabilities, so actually managing your identity, creating the DID document, wrapping that up in verifiable history.

101
00:16:50.690 --> 00:17:04.419
Glenn Gore: managing the keys, the service definitions around that, and then starting to integrate that to DID hosting services, so the entire life cycle of a DID gets abstracted away from you and moved.

102
00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:11.640
Glenn Gore: as we built this, we started to run into first-order problems. And the first-order problem…

103
00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:15.190
Glenn Gore: Was that there was not a…

104
00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:31.000
Glenn Gore: common way of actually defining a task that needed to be done in this world, right? That, you know, I want to register an account, I want to roll the keys in a DID, I want a DID, I don't want the DID anymore.

105
00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:48.039
Glenn Gore: And as we built those out, we had to keep reinventing the wheel for every protocol stack. How I do that with DidCom, how I do that on a REST API, how I do that with TSP, they're all different, and you ended up with all these service endpoints

106
00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:53.380
Glenn Gore: that really, kind of, didn't work as you go through it. And so…

107
00:17:53.470 --> 00:18:09.370
Glenn Gore: what we've built out, and this is kind of just state of the nation right now, is we have our VTA, which is still doing all of that. We have the DIDCOM mediator relay, so it's just an asynchronous message

108
00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:14.440
Glenn Gore: system that is directly tied into the DID,

109
00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:31.690
Glenn Gore: model of thinking, so it's using all the public key information that's encoded within the DIDs and service definitions. We now have a browser wallet plugin for VTA, and you'll see that in a bit. And then you have the relying parties, which are your third-party

110
00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:37.700
Glenn Gore: Websites or endpoints that are trying to authenticate and authorize

111
00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:53.020
Glenn Gore: you in some matter. And the challenge is, again, if you're kind of paranoid with that context I set, just about every single identity authentication system that's used on the internet sits behind an API.

112
00:18:53.370 --> 00:19:12.169
Glenn Gore: And that API is tied to a domain, and it tends to force all of the observable traffic through gateways. It's why Google loves you logging in with Google. They don't know what you're doing, but they know how often you use it, and what sites you're interacting with based on that information.

113
00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:17.609
Glenn Gore: So, what if you could have a world where you could have your VTA,

114
00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:35.939
Glenn Gore: who cares where it runs? Accessible publicly from anywhere, without being publicly known, or tied to a domain, and you can move it around as often as you like, you can roll the DID, you can roll the keys, you can kind of be,

115
00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:48.150
Glenn Gore: you know, as censorship-resistant as you possibly want in this world as you go through it. So they're the four pieces you'll hear kind of coming up a fair bit. We have…

116
00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:53.500
Glenn Gore: the concept of trust tasks. So…

117
00:19:53.860 --> 00:20:00.570
Glenn Gore: to solve this was an encapsulation of this simple concept that Party A

118
00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:20.819
Glenn Gore: it wants Party B to complete a task. That task needs to be provable after the fact, and I want some signed artifact back the other way, saying, did it succeed, did it not, or is there something else I need to go with this? And by doing this, we…

119
00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:33.840
Glenn Gore: basically separate out the concept of what a task is from the protocol that's delivering the task, which is a big… sounds simple, but it's actually quite a big step from an architectural perspective.

120
00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:48.510
Glenn Gore: And so we support… it looks suspiciously like a verified credential. It uses the same data integrity proofs. It's generic enough in that it doesn't really matter what you're using.

121
00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:57.230
Glenn Gore: you know, whether you're using DIDs, what DIDs, VIDs, X509, who cares, it kind of works with anything.

122
00:20:57.430 --> 00:21:05.029
Glenn Gore: We have it implemented across two transports already, REST and DITCOM.

123
00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:22.730
Glenn Gore: those of you that have built APIs in the past, one of the challenges is you end up with kind of an API endpoint for every task that you do, you know, slash account slash delete, slash account slash list, slash account slash get, slash account slash new, and that becomes…

124
00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:25.849
Glenn Gore: Quite unwieldy at scale.

125
00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:39.049
Glenn Gore: in… with Trust Tasks, you actually end up with one API. So, Trust Tasks, or in the DIDCOM world, if you understand how the protocols work, you can just have a single, envelope type.

126
00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:51.039
Glenn Gore: and then sort out the trust tasks underneath this. The second thing it does in the envelope layer is there is the concept of implied proof, not direct proof.

127
00:21:51.180 --> 00:22:02.829
Glenn Gore: So, if you are delivering content over a standard REST API, even though it's protected by SSL or TLS, that's not proof of ownership of

128
00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:22.509
Glenn Gore: who actually created it or owned it. But if you deliver it via a signed DIBCOM message, there is proof that the sender is who they say they are as you go through this. And so, instead of having to double sign things, you can imply proof from an outer wrapper if it meets. And so, there's a lot of, kind of.

129
00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:33.789
Glenn Gore: tech in allowing how that works as you go through it. This is now being used, so VTA has been switched across to using Trust Tasks, it's working really well.

130
00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:41.770
Glenn Gore: The URI, if you've looked at other specs, there's nothing really unique about it. It uses standard URIs.

131
00:22:41.770 --> 00:22:54.770
Glenn Gore: There's a namespace, there's an operations path, and then just a major-minor version number as you go through this. There's over 70 trust tasks defined already, and I'll show you what they look like.

132
00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:57.540
Glenn Gore: But really, kind of.

133
00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:08.769
Glenn Gore: works well as we've been doing breaking changes. Being able to use the URIs and the version numbers is actually working really quite well as we go through this.

134
00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:21.489
Glenn Gore: There's, at the moment, only two return types. There will be a third one. It's either a success, and it's a generic success, and all responses,

135
00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:23.459
Glenn Gore: that are okay.

136
00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:35.069
Glenn Gore: always just actually the URL with a hash response fragment on the end. So, really simple, you don't need to define another, type, it's just implied that it's got hash response, it's there.

137
00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:50.819
Glenn Gore: Or is an error reject, and the error reject is its own type and gives a lot of metadata if you want it. It is okay to silently fail, but most tasks, you probably want something to come back saying what it is.

138
00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:53.890
Glenn Gore: I'll share this. So…

139
00:23:53.940 --> 00:24:13.020
Glenn Gore: To give you an example, this is the auth challenge, so what you'll see when I show you the authentication challenge is it's actually made up of three trust tasks. That's it. That is all you need to do authentication in this world. You can see auth challenge on the left, it's pretty simple.

140
00:24:13.210 --> 00:24:19.909
Glenn Gore: Who's the audience that will come through this? And the response is just a hash response, and some information coming back.

141
00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:42.450
Glenn Gore: The authenticate, auth slash authenticate, is actually the most, used trust task in the VTI landscape already. The mediator uses it, VTA uses it, did hosting services use it, OpenVTC uses it, PNM, VTC, CNM, they all use it. It's like Dr. Seuss acronym salad there.

142
00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:43.899
Glenn Gore: As you go through this.

143
00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:54.769
Glenn Gore: The specs themselves, so the trust tasks, they are, if you look at the raw file, it's a kind of markdown language.

144
00:24:54.830 --> 00:24:57.440
Glenn Gore: But they are meant…

145
00:24:57.520 --> 00:25:06.189
Glenn Gore: to be read by machines, and they get translated to a human view as you go through it. So, for example.

146
00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:10.930
Glenn Gore: If we look back at this… where's our…

